WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.307
Here's a question that sounds simple.

00:00:02.347 --> 00:00:04.679
Five clinics send their patients home.

00:00:04.719 --> 00:00:08.612
Some of those patients are back
in hospital within thirty days.

00:00:08.652 --> 00:00:11.187
Which clinic is doing the best job?

00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:16.270
Each chip is one patient. A filled
chip is a patient who was readmitted.

00:00:16.417 --> 00:00:23.417
The obvious move is to count. At Alder,
13 of 30 patients came back: that's 43%.

00:00:23.457 --> 00:00:30.186
Birch, 47%. Cedar, 40%. Dune,
58%. And at Elm, nobody at all.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:34.026
Line them up from lowest to
highest, and we have a league table.

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:38.363
Elm in first place, Dune in last,
and Alder just ahead of Birch.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.010
It looks like an answer. But for
those two, the table has it backwards.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.136
Welcome to Model in Motion,

00:00:46.176 --> 00:00:50.241
where we build statistical models
one moving piece at a time.

00:00:50.281 --> 00:00:54.601
This is the start of a new story:
how to compare providers fairly.

00:00:54.641 --> 00:00:58.823
Today, unfair comparisons, and
what a fair one would need.

00:01:00.717 --> 00:01:03.344
Let's look more closely at Alder and Birch.

00:01:03.384 --> 00:01:08.192
So far, we've treated every patient
as interchangeable. They aren't.

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.350
Some are frail, or going home
after a long and complicated stay.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:17.360
Patients like that are more likely to
come back, wherever they're treated.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.331
Let's mark those higher-risk
patients with a ring.

00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:25.340
Now sort the chips: lower-risk patients
on the left, higher-risk on the right.

00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:31.864
Look at who each clinic is caring for. At
Alder, 24 of the 30 patients are lower-risk.

00:01:31.904 --> 00:01:36.436
Birch is the mirror image:
24 of its 30 are higher-risk.

00:01:36.583 --> 00:01:39.957
This is a clinic's case mix:
the kind of patients it serves.

00:01:39.997 --> 00:01:42.906
And these two could hardly be more different.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.934
So let's compare like with like,
starting with the lower-risk patients.

00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:55.574
At Alder, 8 of 24 were readmitted: one
in three. At Birch, 1 of 6: about 17%.

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:58.268
For these patients, Birch does better.

00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:07.358
Now the higher-risk patients. Alder: 5
of 6, or 83%. Birch: 13 of 24, or 54%.

00:02:07.398 --> 00:02:09.263
Birch does better again.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:14.416
Lower-risk patients do better at Birch.
Higher-risk patients do better at Birch.

00:02:14.456 --> 00:02:18.753
And yet, put everyone back together,
and it's Alder with the lower rate.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.925
Nothing is wrong with the arithmetic.
This is Simpson's paradox,

00:02:22.965 --> 00:02:27.762
and it lies in wait wherever groups
with different mixes are compared.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.901
Here's what's going on.
Give each clinic a beam,

00:02:31.941 --> 00:02:34.713
marked from zero to one hundred percent.

00:02:34.753 --> 00:02:39.156
Each group of patients sits on the
beam at its own readmission rate.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.084
A clinic's overall rate is the
point where its beam balances.

00:02:43.124 --> 00:02:46.797
And that depends on how much
weight sits at each end.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:53.534
Alder's patients are mostly lower-risk, so
its balance point sits near the low end: 43%.

00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:58.144
Birch's piles both sit further
left: lower rates in both groups.

00:02:58.184 --> 00:03:01.553
But most of its weight is
at the higher-risk end,

00:03:01.593 --> 00:03:04.934
and that drags the balance point up to 47%.

00:03:05.083 --> 00:03:10.574
So a raw rate blends two things: how well a
clinic performs, and who walked in the door.

00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:16.390
Here's a way to feel the difference.
Imagine the two clinics traded patients.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:22.674
Alder's two rates stay where they were.
But now it carries Birch's patients,

00:03:22.714 --> 00:03:26.515
and its balance point slides
all the way up to 73%.

00:03:26.555 --> 00:03:30.020
With those same patients, Birch managed 47%.

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:37.429
And Birch, with Alder's easier mix? Its balance
point falls to 24%, where Alder had 43%.

00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:41.292
Whichever set of patients you
choose, Birch comes out well ahead.

00:03:41.332 --> 00:03:44.637
The league table had these
two the wrong way round.

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:50.338
Two clinics could be a fluke. So
let's zoom out to 200 providers.

00:03:50.378 --> 00:03:54.632
They're simulated, which means we
know how good each one truly is.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:59.036
Line them up from truly best on the
left to truly worst on the right.

00:03:59.076 --> 00:04:04.290
Then lift each one by its raw rate:
lowest at the bottom, highest at the top.

00:04:04.433 --> 00:04:09.582
If raw rates told the truth, the
dots would hug the diagonal. Instead,

00:04:09.622 --> 00:04:15.087
they're scattered almost everywhere:
a rank correlation of just 0.16.

00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:21.684
Cut the table into fifths, and 142 of
the 200 providers land in the wrong one.

00:04:21.833 --> 00:04:26.444
Now re-sort the very same providers
by how sick their patients are.

00:04:26.484 --> 00:04:32.700
There's the pattern the raw rates were
following all along: a correlation of 0.58.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.859
There's a second problem in that league table,

00:04:36.899 --> 00:04:40.922
and it has nothing to do with case
mix. Look at the two extremes.

00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:46.985
Dune's 58% rests on 12 patients.
Elm's perfect zero, on 11.

00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:51.979
So try an experiment. Suppose all
five clinics were secretly identical:

00:04:52.019 --> 00:04:56.246
every patient with the same
four-in-ten chance of coming back.

00:04:56.286 --> 00:04:59.594
Now deal out the luck. And again. And again.

00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:03.504
Even the bigger clinics wobble.
The smallest swing much further.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.308
So before trusting any gap between providers,

00:05:06.348 --> 00:05:09.973
we'll have to ask how much of
it chance alone could produce.

00:05:10.013 --> 00:05:12.867
That question gets an episode of its own.

00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:15.963
Let's gather it up.

00:05:16.003 --> 00:05:18.950
A raw rate blends performance with case mix.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.851
Compare like with like,
and a ranking can flip.

00:05:21.891 --> 00:05:24.289
And small providers swing with chance.

00:05:24.433 --> 00:05:27.470
What we really want is to
compare providers as if they

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.726
had all treated the same patients.

00:05:29.766 --> 00:05:33.765
With one risk factor, sorting
chips into two piles was enough.

00:05:33.805 --> 00:05:37.476
But real patients differ
in dozens of ways at once.

00:05:37.617 --> 00:05:40.768
So next time, we'll build
a single ruler for risk:

00:05:40.808 --> 00:05:45.626
a way to turn everything we know
about a patient into one probability.

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:49.872
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
